Project 1: My little malloc()
Authors:
  Radha Ghate ()
  Zeal Shah ([netID])

------------------------------------------------------------
Design Notes
------------------------------------------------------------

Header layout
  Each chunk has an 8-byte header stored as a single size_t.
  All payload sizes are multiples of 8, so the low 3 bits of
  the size are always 0. We steal bit 0 as the allocated flag:

      stored_value = payload_size | alloc_flag

  GET_SIZE masks the low bits off. GET_ALLOC reads bit 0.
  This keeps the header exactly 8 bytes, so the minimum chunk
  size is 16 bytes (8 header + 8 payload).

Allocation
  We use first-fit: walk the chunk list from the start of the
  heap and take the first free chunk that is large enough. If
  the leftover space after carving out the request is at least
  16 bytes (the minimum chunk size) we split it into a second
  free chunk. All request sizes are rounded up to the nearest
  multiple of 8 before the search. If the first scan finds
  nothing we run a full coalesce pass and try one more time.

Coalescing
  free() always runs a full forward-coalesce pass after marking
  a chunk free. This merges consecutive free chunks into one
  regardless of the order they were freed in. malloc() also
  triggers a coalesce pass as a last resort before giving up
  and returning NULL.

Error detection
  myfree() validates the pointer before touching any chunk:
    1. Pointer must be inside the heap bounds
    2. Pointer must be 8-byte aligned
    3. Heap walk must confirm it lands exactly at a chunk
       payload start (catches mid-chunk pointers)
    4. That chunk must be currently allocated (catches
       double-frees)
  All four checks print to stderr and call exit(2) on failure.

Leak detection
  init_heap() registers leak_detect() with atexit(). When the
  process exits, leak_detect() walks the heap, counts chunks
  that are still allocated, and prints the total byte count
  and object count to stderr.

------------------------------------------------------------
Test Plan
------------------------------------------------------------

memtest.c  (provided)
  Allocates exactly 64 objects that fill the 4096-byte heap,
  writes a distinct byte pattern to each one, and checks that
  no object was overwritten by a neighbor. Run with -DLEAK to
  skip the frees and trigger the leak detector.

  Expected:  ./memtest       -> "0 incorrect bytes"
             ./memtest-leak  -> leak report on stderr

memgrind.c  (stress and timing)
  Runs 5 workloads 50 times each and prints the average time
  per run. All tasks free every object they allocate.

  Task 1: malloc and immediately free a 1-byte object, 120 times
  Task 2: malloc 120 1-byte objects into an array, then free all
  Task 3: random mix of malloc and free until 120 total
          allocations, then free whatever remains
  Task 4: allocate 30 objects of varying sizes (1-8 bytes),
          then free them in reverse order
  Task 5: queue simulation -- fill 60 slots, then repeatedly
          dequeue from the front and enqueue at the back

  Expected:  ./memgrind  -> timing output for all 5 tasks,
                            no errors on stderr

tests/test1.c  -- allocation and data integrity
  Allocates objects of size 8, 16, 32, and 64 bytes. Checks
  that every returned pointer is 8-byte aligned. Writes a
  distinct byte pattern to each object and verifies that no
  object was corrupted by a neighbor. Also tests alloc-after-
  free, malloc(0) returning NULL, and free(NULL) being a no-op.

  Expected:  ./test1  -> "Test 1 passed!"

tests/test2.c  -- free() releases memory for reuse
  Fills the entire heap with 128 objects, confirms the next
  malloc fails (heap is full), frees everything, then fills
  the heap a second time. If free() doesn't actually release
  memory the second fill will fail immediately. Also checks
  data integrity of the second fill.

  Expected:  ./test2  -> "Test 2 passed!"

tests/test3.c  -- coalescing
  Frees the same set of chunks in four different orders:
  left-to-right, right-to-left, middle-first, and alternating.
  After each pattern it requests a block larger than any single
  freed chunk. This only succeeds if adjacent free chunks were
  properly merged.

  Expected:  ./test3  -> "Test 3 passed!"

tests/test4.c  -- edge cases
  Checks that malloc(10000) returns NULL (bigger than the heap),
  that malloc(4096) returns NULL (no room for the header), that
  non-multiples-of-8 request sizes are handled without crashing,
  that all returned pointers are 8-byte aligned across sizes
  1-16, and that 20 repeated alloc/free cycles leave the heap
  consistent.

  Expected:  ./test4  -> "Test 4 passed!"

tests/test5.c  -- error: free() on a stack variable
  Calls free(&x) where x is a local int. The address is outside
  the heap so myfree() catches it on the bounds check.

  Expected:  ./test5  -> error on stderr, exit code 2

tests/test6.c  -- error: free() on a mid-chunk pointer
  Allocates an int array and calls free(p + 1). The pointer is
  4 bytes past the payload start, not at any chunk boundary.

  Expected:  ./test6  -> error on stderr, exit code 2

tests/test7.c  -- error: double free
  Allocates one block, stores the pointer in both p and q,
  frees p, then frees q. The second free hits a chunk that is
  already marked free.

  Expected:  ./test7  -> error on stderr, exit code 2

tests/test8.c  -- leak detection
  Allocates three objects (100, 200, 50 bytes) and never frees
  them. The atexit() leak detector reports them at exit.

  Expected:  ./test8  -> exits 0; stderr shows
                         "mymalloc: NNN bytes leaked in 3 objects."

------------------------------------------------------------
Running the tests
------------------------------------------------------------

  make                 # compile everything

  ./memtest            # 0 incorrect bytes
  ./memgrind           # timing output for all 5 tasks
  ./test1              # Test 1 passed!
  ./test2              # Test 2 passed!
  ./test3              # Test 3 passed!
  ./test4              # Test 4 passed!
  ./test8              # check stderr for leak report

  make test            # run all of the above together

  # these exit with code 2 -- run separately or via:
  make test-errors
  ./test5              # free() on stack variable
  ./test6              # free() on mid-chunk pointer
  ./test7              # double free

------------------------------------------------------------
Expected results summary
------------------------------------------------------------

  memtest:        "0 incorrect bytes"
  memgrind:       timing lines for all 5 tasks, no stderr output
  test1-test4:    "Test N passed!"
  test8:          "mymalloc: NNN bytes leaked in 3 objects." on stderr
  test5,6,7:      "free: Inappropriate pointer ..." on stderr, exit 2
